WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.968
-Our first guest is

00:00:01.968 --> 00:00:03.335
an Emmy-nominated actress
and writer you know from

00:00:03.335 --> 00:00:05.004
her hit HBO series, "Insecure."

00:00:05.004 --> 00:00:08.508
She both executive produced
and stars in "The Lovebirds,"

00:00:08.508 --> 00:00:10.210
which is available this Friday
on Netflix.

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:13.112
Here's my conversation
with Issa Rae.

00:00:13.112 --> 00:00:15.315
-Welcome back to the show, Issa.
How are you?

00:00:15.315 --> 00:00:17.783
-I am doing great.
How you doing?

00:00:17.783 --> 00:00:18.951
-I'm doing very well.

00:00:18.951 --> 00:00:20.286
Do you have
a quarantine schedule?

00:00:20.286 --> 00:00:21.954
Do you have a way
through the day?

00:00:21.954 --> 00:00:23.423
-That's the only way
that I'm surviving.

00:00:23.423 --> 00:00:26.559
I'm such a routine person,
in general.

00:00:26.559 --> 00:00:29.529
So this has been like
heaven and hell simultaneously.

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:30.830
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:33.833
-So as long as I keep that
routine, I'm all right.

00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:35.835
-Like a lot of people,
I've been listening

00:00:35.835 --> 00:00:37.403
to a fair amount of podcasts
through this.

00:00:37.403 --> 00:00:40.573
And I heard you on a podcast
called "The Rewatchables"

00:00:40.573 --> 00:00:43.075
with Bill Simmons where you were
talking about "Groundhog Day"

00:00:43.075 --> 00:00:44.444
is one of your favorite movies.

00:00:44.444 --> 00:00:47.746
Do you feel as though
you're living through that now?

00:00:47.746 --> 00:00:50.583
-Yeah, definitely.
I think -- you know, which is --

00:00:50.583 --> 00:00:52.384
That's one of
the greatest movies of all time,

00:00:52.384 --> 00:00:53.719
one of my favorite movies,

00:00:53.719 --> 00:00:55.487
and it was such a treat
to talk about that.

00:00:55.487 --> 00:00:58.557
But it is like you're waking up
with the same alarm

00:00:58.557 --> 00:01:01.894
every single day, going to
the same parts of your house.

00:01:01.894 --> 00:01:06.532
The only sad part is that there
are consequences for my actions.

00:01:06.532 --> 00:01:08.501
-Yeah, of course.
[ Both laugh ]

00:01:08.501 --> 00:01:09.969
Were you always --

00:01:09.969 --> 00:01:13.038
Have you always been
a big Bill Murray fan?

00:01:13.038 --> 00:01:14.340
-I have.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:18.111
I mean, I grew up
on "Ghostbusters."

00:01:18.111 --> 00:01:19.646
Yeah, he's just
always been a staple.

00:01:19.646 --> 00:01:21.280
Seeing him on
"Saturday Night Live,"

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:24.149
obviously, he's just --
he's a treat.

00:01:24.149 --> 00:01:25.484
-He is a treat.

00:01:25.484 --> 00:01:28.555
Another treat
is Kumail Nanjiani,

00:01:28.555 --> 00:01:30.623
and you guys
did this film together.

00:01:30.623 --> 00:01:32.658
It was supposed to premiere
at South by Southwest.

00:01:32.658 --> 00:01:35.328
Of course that was one of the
many things that was cancelled.

00:01:35.328 --> 00:01:37.363
Now it's gonna premiere
on Netflix.

00:01:37.363 --> 00:01:39.065
Can you tell us
a little bit about the movie?

00:01:39.065 --> 00:01:43.169
-Yeah, the movie is about
a couple that is --

00:01:43.169 --> 00:01:46.806
kind of reached a stagnant point
in their relationship

00:01:46.806 --> 00:01:52.011
and are going out on,
you know, a night --

00:01:52.011 --> 00:01:53.613
They're going on a night out.

00:01:53.613 --> 00:01:57.683
And they are framed for murder,
like those things happen.

00:01:57.683 --> 00:01:59.385
-[ Laughs ]
It's good, though.

00:01:59.385 --> 00:02:02.288
Sometimes a couple
needs to be framed for murder

00:02:02.288 --> 00:02:03.922
to appreciate one another.

00:02:03.922 --> 00:02:05.024
-Just to spice things up.

00:02:05.024 --> 00:02:06.592
Like, you know,
things get boring.

00:02:06.592 --> 00:02:08.227
And I want to know
what you're like

00:02:08.227 --> 00:02:10.596
when our lives are on the line.

00:02:10.596 --> 00:02:14.099
-There is --
Theres a horse in the film.

00:02:14.099 --> 00:02:15.935
This was not a real horse

00:02:15.935 --> 00:02:17.103
throughout the course
of the film.

00:02:17.103 --> 00:02:19.538
There was some
fake horsing happening.

00:02:19.538 --> 00:02:22.241
-There was some fake horsery,
for our safety --

00:02:22.241 --> 00:02:24.643
for Kumail's safety.
-Yeah.

00:02:24.643 --> 00:02:28.948
-You know -- you see him, in the
trailer, gets kicked by a horse.

00:02:28.948 --> 00:02:32.218
And I made sure,
when the real horse was there,

00:02:32.218 --> 00:02:35.521
that I kept distance from it.

00:02:35.521 --> 00:02:37.523
-I'm like you 100%.

00:02:37.523 --> 00:02:39.392
I never feel comfortable
around horses.

00:02:39.392 --> 00:02:42.494
I don't -- not even --
Have you ridden on a horse?

00:02:42.494 --> 00:02:45.097
Is that something that seems
relaxing to you at all?

00:02:45.097 --> 00:02:46.799
-No.
Relaxing? No.

00:02:46.799 --> 00:02:50.102
I've done the side horse thing,
but even that,

00:02:50.102 --> 00:02:51.837
someone was holding my hand
and guiding me.

00:02:51.837 --> 00:02:53.539
But no.
Have you ridden a horse?

00:02:53.539 --> 00:02:55.240
-Yeah.
I have ridden a horse,

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:56.675
and I hated every second of it.

00:02:56.675 --> 00:02:59.411
And it's not --
I mean, like, I --

00:02:59.411 --> 00:03:04.083
I just don't like that another
brain is in charge of my speed.

00:03:04.083 --> 00:03:06.486
-Exactly.
And you're just supposed to

00:03:06.486 --> 00:03:09.622
give your faith to this --
It's an animal.

00:03:09.622 --> 00:03:10.690
-Yeah.
-And there's not even like --

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:12.192
You know,
there's no reasoning with it,

00:03:12.192 --> 00:03:14.160
so it can do whatever
it feels like.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.162
So it's horrifying.

00:03:16.162 --> 00:03:19.565
-A lot of attention,
for my money, correctly so,

00:03:19.565 --> 00:03:23.069
has been paid to the
transformation of Kumail,

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:25.704
who has for a long, long time

00:03:25.704 --> 00:03:27.340
been one of
the funniest people working.

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:29.742
And then, he got cast
in a Marvel project

00:03:29.742 --> 00:03:33.112
and now has the most
super-ripped body of all time.

00:03:33.112 --> 00:03:34.947
I'm wondering if,
on set for this film,

00:03:34.947 --> 00:03:39.385
which was before that,
he shot his Marvel project,

00:03:39.385 --> 00:03:43.489
was Kumail very, I imagine,
conscious to what he was eating?

00:03:43.489 --> 00:03:44.791
-He was conscious.

00:03:44.791 --> 00:03:46.625
He had just started the journey.

00:03:46.625 --> 00:03:49.695
And, to me, that's heaven,
because I'm a fad diet,

00:03:49.695 --> 00:03:52.465
crash diet person,
so I'm always doing something.

00:03:52.465 --> 00:03:56.436
So to meet a guy who was just as
food conscious as I was --

00:03:56.436 --> 00:03:57.670
That doesn't mean I do well.

00:03:57.670 --> 00:03:59.438
I just like
to try different things.

00:03:59.438 --> 00:04:01.340
-Sure, and it's nice to have
a partner for that journey.

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:03.208
-It's nice to have
an accountability partner.

00:04:03.208 --> 00:04:06.044
But he really just
took it to another level,

00:04:06.044 --> 00:04:08.715
and I'm extremely jealous
of him.

